== Buildcraft at the Endgame

Wave 1 taught you to spend skills. Endgame buildcraft is about *assembling* — several complete loadouts, each for a job.

=== The Loadout Library

Maintain four archetypes and adapt them per matchup:

- *Damage set* — Weakness Exploit + affinity + attack; for fights you know cold.
- *Comfort set* — Evade Window, Divine Blessing, Stun Resistance; for new monsters and long sessions.
- *Elemental set* — the right element at high value, for monsters with glaring weaknesses.
- *Support set* — for multiplayer: buffs, healing, wide-range item sharing (see *Hunt Etiquette*).

Each gets a save slot in your equipment box. Adjust per hunt, not from scratch.

=== The Endgame Priorities

- *Affinity first.* Critical skills scale with everything else; the damage floor rises with them.
- *Then raw/element, then utility.* Once your crits are consistent, flat attack and elemental values compound.
- *Slots are king.* MR armor with generous slots beats slightly-better skills with none — slots accept any skill you need for the matchup.
- *Never skip the comfort core* in new fights. A set that lets you stay in the fight deals more damage than a set that carts you.

=== Augments & Talismans: The Long Game

- Your *talisman* defines the build's skeleton: roll with whichever melding recipe targets your weapon family, and let the talisman's skills dictate which armor pieces to swap.
- Augments close the last gaps: +1 skill here, +1 slot there. Spend them last, on the weapon you are sure about.
- Accept that some builds wait for a talisman you do not own yet. Play the best version now; upgrade the set when luck lands.

=== Reading Your Own Build

Before each hunt, ask three questions:

1. *Can I survive this monster's enrage?* — if no, add comfort.
2. *Am I hitting its weak spot?* — if yes, add damage; if no, fix positioning, not gear.
3. *Does this set exist for this fight, or am I forcing it?* — a wrong element is a 10-minute drag; swapping sets takes one minute.

#tip[
  Endgame buildcraft is a feedback loop: fight, audit, adjust. The build that never changes is the build that stopped improving — and the monsters you face next will notice.
]
